Chapter: C64IoMap
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites C64 chapter Memory
  cites C64 chapter Cia

 The SID and the two CIAs keep their register state in fixed heap blocks, and
 the SID block and the CIA block were both at #A16000. The SID owns 32 bytes;
 the CIA block holds the keyboard matrix at 0 to 7, CIA1 at 16 to 31 and CIA2
 at 32 to 47, so every SID register aliased a CIA byte.

 Row 1: a write to SID voice 1 frequency lo (#D400) must not press keys in
 keyboard column 0, which `cia-init` leaves at #FF (no key down).
 Row 2: a write to CIA1 port A (#DC00) must not change SID register 16 (#D410).
 Row 3: the control, each device reads back what was written to it.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let i = cia-init 0
    in let w1 = c64-write-io-reg #D400 0
    in let col0 = peek-byte cia-state 0
    in let w2 = c64-write-io-reg #D410 0
    in let w3 = c64-write-io-reg #DC00 #7F
    in let sid16 = c64-read-io-reg #D410
    in let w4 = c64-write-io-reg #D418 15
    in act
      print-line-uni ("1-sid-vs-matrix col0=" & show col0)
      print-line-uni ("2-cia-vs-sid sid16=" & show sid16)
      print-line-uni ("3-control sid24=" & show (c64-read-io-reg #D418) & " cia-porta=" & show (c64-read-io-reg #DC00))
    end

